---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const baseURL = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/?$/, '/');
---
<BaseLayout title="About Marcelo Ellwanger | Engineering Portfolio" description="Professional background, engineering achievements, current work and career focus from Marcelo Ellwanger.">
  <section class="about-hero wrap">
    <div class="about-hero-copy">
      <p class="eyebrow">About Marcelo · Professional profile</p>
      <h1>I build useful systems, then make them trustworthy.</h1>
      <p class="about-lead">I’m Marcelo Ellwanger, a multilingual builder with a non-traditional engineering path across electrical work, operations management, international customer-facing roles and practical software development. I like understanding how systems behave, finding what is inefficient or unclear, and building something better around the real constraints.</p>
      <div class="case-actions">
        <a class="button primary" href="/resume/software/">Software / QA résumé ↗</a>
        <a class="button" href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/marcelo-ellwanger-10657a435/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">LinkedIn ↗</a>
        <a class="button" href="/contact/">Contact ↗</a>
        <a class="button" href="https://github.com/marcelosofficial-ctrl" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">GitHub ↗</a>
      </div>
      <div class="about-factline" aria-label="Professional facts">
        <span>Based in Japan</span>
        <span>Remote / global</span>
        <span>5-language profile</span>
        <span>4,000+ trade hours</span>
      </div>
      <blockquote class="about-signature">My route into software wasn’t linear. The common thread was: understand the system, find the fault, and leave it better than I found it.</blockquote>
    </div>

    <aside class="about-profile-panel" aria-label="Marcelo Ellwanger professional profile">
      <figure class="about-portrait">
        <img src={`${baseURL}profile/marcelo-profile.webp`} alt="Professional portrait of Marcelo Ellwanger" width="260" height="347" loading="eager" decoding="async" />
        <figcaption class="mono"><span>MARCELO ELLWANGER</span><small>JAPAN · 2026</small></figcaption>
      </figure>
      <div class="about-card mono">
        <div><span>FOCUS</span><strong>Software · Systems · Automation</strong></div>
        <div><span>WORK STYLE</span><strong>Evidence-first</strong></div>
        <div><span>LANGUAGES</span><strong>5</strong></div>
        <div><span>BACKGROUND</span><strong>Technical + operations + customer-facing</strong></div>
        <div><span>CURRENT</span><strong>CrashScope beta ready · EasyFLix active</strong></div>
        <div><span>AVAILABILITY</span><strong>Remote · global time zones</strong></div>
      </div>
    </aside>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section wrap about-story">
    <div class="case-section-heading"><p class="eyebrow">The path</p><h2>A hands-on route into software.</h2></div>
    <div class="about-story-grid">
      <article><span class="mono">01 / TRADE</span><h3>Electrical work</h3><p>I spent years doing electrical work in Canada, accumulating roughly 4,000 hours of hands-on experience with electrical and mechanical equipment, high- and low-voltage wiring, troubleshooting and work that had to be correct in the physical world.</p></article>
      <article><span class="mono">02 / OPERATIONS</span><h3>Coffee management in Surfers Paradise</h3><p>At Alma on the Gold Coast, I managed the coffee side of an upscale all-day venue: employee scheduling, stock ordering, inventory awareness and specialty-coffee quality. Recipes and calibration were treated precisely, with professional baristas, premium equipment and measurements down to dose, yield and extraction time.</p></article>
      <article><span class="mono">03 / SOFTWARE</span><h3>Building the tools I wanted</h3><p>Software became the place where those instincts converged. I started solving problems I actually had: local AI workflows, resale research, unstable PCs, media organization and small desktop interfaces. Those projects became increasingly rigorous products rather than throwaway scripts.</p></article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="about-manifesto">
    <div class="wrap about-manifesto-inner">
      <div class="about-manifesto-label mono">PERSONAL / ENGINEERING NOTE</div>
      <p>I’m most interested in software where the messy parts matter: <em>real hardware, imperfect data, human workflows and failures that need explaining.</em></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section wrap">
    <div class="case-section-heading"><p class="eyebrow">Selected achievements</p><h2>Things I can point to, not just claim.</h2></div>
    <div class="achievement-grid">
      <article><span class="achievement-kicker mono">WINDOWS DIAGNOSTICS</span><strong>CrashScope v0.1 beta ready</strong><p>Built and validated a local-first Windows crash-diagnostics system with 185 automated .NET tests, a native tray, Auto Assist / Everyday Mode, persistent sessions and incidents, Event Log + WER evidence, and privacy-safe support bundles. Final integrated Agent validation measured 0.2657% average CPU, 93 MB average and 95.83 MB peak working set on Ryzen 5 7500F + Radeon RX 9070 XT.</p></article>
      <article><span class="achievement-kicker mono">SHIPPED PRODUCT</span><strong>VektorDeck 1.0</strong><p>Designed, built and released a local-first AI workstation manager with runtime safety, GGUF Model Intelligence, telemetry, Smart Launch recommendations, CI and live post-update smoke validation.</p></article>
      <article><span class="achievement-kicker mono">FIELD-TESTED SYSTEM</span><strong>Retro Game Vision</strong><p>Built a provenance-first photo-to-decision resale pipeline that grew to hundreds of canonical items and more than a thousand physical observations while preserving evidence lineage and uncertainty.</p></article>
      <article><span class="achievement-kicker mono">ACTIVE WINDOWS APP</span><strong>EasyFLix · pre-1.0 ReelShelf</strong><p>Building a non-destructive WPF/.NET 10 local-media application with read-only multi-drive scanning, 48 automated tests, zero build warnings and portable win-x64 validation.</p></article>
      <article><span class="achievement-kicker mono">NON-TRADITIONAL EXPERIENCE</span><strong>4,000+ trade hours</strong><p>Years of electrical work taught me to value failure modes, reproducibility, safety and systems that behave correctly outside a controlled demo.</p></article>
      <article><span class="achievement-kicker mono">COMMUNICATION</span><strong>Five-language profile</strong><p>International work across Canada, Australia and Japan developed a communication style built around adapting quickly, explaining clearly and working with people from different backgrounds.</p></article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section wrap">
    <div class="case-section-heading"><p class="eyebrow">Current engineering focus</p><h2>What I’m building now.</h2></div>
    <div class="current-focus-grid">
      <a href="/projects/crashscope/"><span class="mono">V0.1 BETA READY / WINDOWS SYSTEMS</span><strong>CrashScope</strong><p>Validated local-first Windows crash diagnostics with persistent incidents, Event Log + WER evidence, native tray controls and privacy-safe support bundles. First public beta release is in preparation.</p><b>Open case study ↗</b></a>
      <a href="/projects/reelshelf/"><span class="mono">ACTIVE / WINDOWS LOCAL MEDIA</span><strong>EasyFLix</strong><p>Current pre-1.0 working title: ReelShelf. A WPF/.NET 10 desktop application for legally acquired local media with read-only multi-drive scanning, unified shortcuts, artwork and quality-gated subtitle workflows that leave source media untouched.</p><b>Open active case study ↗</b></a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section wrap about-goals">
    <div><p class="eyebrow">Goals</p><h2>I want to keep building software that earns trust.</h2></div>
    <div class="about-goal-copy">
      <p>Professionally, I’m targeting software engineering, QA / automation and technical systems roles where practical debugging, reliability and systems thinking matter. Local software, developer tools, automation, data systems and diagnostics are especially natural fits.</p>
      <p>I’m also open to technical support or operations roles where strong communication, troubleshooting and independent work are central to the job. I can work flexibly across global time zones and schedules.</p>
      <p>I want this site to work as a portfolio for employers, a professional introduction for collaborators and clients, and eventually the URL or QR code I can put on a business card without needing a second explanation.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section wrap">
    <div class="case-section-heading"><p class="eyebrow">How I work</p><h2>The principles behind the projects.</h2></div>
    <div class="skill-matrix">
      <div><span>Start with reality</span><p>I prefer a real messy problem over a clean tutorial prompt. Constraints reveal the architecture.</p></div>
      <div><span>Keep the evidence</span><p>Data provenance, explicit uncertainty and reproducible validation matter more than a confident-looking answer.</p></div>
      <div><span>Make failure useful</span><p>A bug should become a test, a clearer boundary or a product rule rather than something quietly patched around.</p></div>
      <div><span>Respect the machine</span><p>Monitoring, AI tooling and background automation should be conscious of CPU, RAM, GPU and user control.</p></div>
      <div><span>Build for maintenance</span><p>Update, repair, diagnostics, idempotence and clear project state are part of the product, not post-release chores.</p></div>
      <div><span>Explain the decision</span><p>If software recommends, classifies or diagnoses something, I want the user to understand why.</p></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="about-contact wrap" id="contact">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">Professional use</p>
      <h2>For work, collaboration or a serious project.</h2>
      <p>The website is the public overview; GitHub contains implementations and engineering notes. For remote roles, technical collaborations or project conversations, LinkedIn and the contact page are the current public routes.</p>
      <div class="professional-contact mono">
        <span>PUBLIC EMPLOYER EMAIL · TO BE ADDED</span>
        <span>REMOTE · FLEXIBLE TIME ZONES · AVAILABLE</span>
      </div>
      <div class="case-actions">
        <a class="button primary" href="/contact/">Contact page ↗</a>
        <a class="button" href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/marcelo-ellwanger-10657a435/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">LinkedIn ↗</a>
        <a class="button" href="/resume/software/">Software / QA résumé ↗</a>
        <a class="button" href="https://github.com/marcelosofficial-ctrl" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">GitHub ↗</a>
      </div>
    </div>
    <figure class="portfolio-qr-card">
      <img src={`${baseURL}portfolio-qr.svg`} alt="QR code for Marcelo Ellwanger's portfolio" loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption><span class="mono">PORTFOLIO / QR</span><p>Designed for a future business card, résumé footer or in-person introduction.</p></figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
